+//! The **retirement leg** of the custodian's reconciliation step: the drain of the `retire:`
+//! ledger (proposal 0016 decision 4, `0016:1079-1290`; the *Retirement drain step* row of the
+//! batch inventory, `0016:668`).
+//!
+//! Every multipart writer that removes the last reference to bytes or records installs one small
+//! `retire:` obligation in the same batch (Abort's `{session, all}`, the part commit's per-part
+//! `{chunks}`, the root flip's `{parts}` and `{generation}`, a rollback's `{seg}`). This leg is
+//! what keeps those promises: each pass walks `retire:` in bounded pages and drains each
+//! obligation **one step at a time**. A step reads the obligation, derives the next batch of work
+//! from the obligation's own **cursor** ([`RetireCursor`], `0016:613-619`: the cursor lives inside
+//! the obligation, never in a record of its own), and commits
+//! `require(retire:… == prior)` + the batch + the cursor's advance — or, in the step that finishes
+//! it, the obligation's delete. A crash or a restart resumes from the stored cursor.
+//!
+//! **The two modes are opposite instructions** (`0016:434-441`), read from the key:
+//!
+//! * `retire:bytes:` — **mark, then delete**. Every fragment position the named bytes occupy gets
+//!   an `orphan:` mark naming this obligation's token, and only then is the record that named the
+//!   bytes (a `part:` + `psum:` pair, a `seg:` record) deleted, in the same commit as the last of
+//!   its marks or a later one: evidence precedes the lifting of protection (`0016:1132-1138`). A
+//!   chunk list no record names (`{chunks}`, a flat `{generation}`) is marked and nothing else.
+//! * `retire:records:` — **delete only, never mark**: the bytes are protected by something else
+//!   (a published object, a part record).
+//!
+//! **The mark guard** (`0016:1220-1226`, after the `reclaiming` check `metadata.rs`'s
+//! [`OrphanMark`] states). Each position's mark is read through the one codec
+//! ([`decode_orphan_mark`]), then:
+//!
+//! * **(0)** it is `reclaiming` — GC's recorded decision to reclaim, whatever event it carries:
+//!   it is left byte-identical, the step stops at that position with the cursor at or before it,
+//!   and the obligation stays. GC deletes the key blind once it has destroyed the fragment
+//!   (`crate::gc`, `Sweep::destroy`), so a later pass finds the key gone and takes arm (c). A
+//!   value written over a `reclaiming` mark would vanish with GC's key delete and leave this
+//!   event with no evidence. A step whose arm-(b) re-stamp loses its precondition is never
+//!   retried as (b): the next step re-reads, and a `reclaiming` value is then arm (0);
+//! * **(a)** it names this obligation's event — a duplicate of this retirement: skipped, its
+//!   stamp intact;
+//! * **(b)** it names another event, or none (a legacy mark): stale evidence, re-stamped under the
+//!   exact-value `require(== prior)` with this event and a fresh `orphaned_at`;
+//! * **(c)** it is absent: written under `require_absent`.
+//!
+//! A mark that is none of the three shapes is left byte-identical and holds the obligation, as
+//! GC keeps it (ADR-0045 decision 3).
+//!
+//! **What the drain does not trust, it holds** (ADR-0045 decision 3). A staged record whose
+//! placement is not exactly one D server per fragment ([`gc::staged_placement`]), one that will
+//! not decode, a committed chunk whose placement is malformed, a key that is not one of its range's
+//! records, an obligation that will not decode against its key: the step neither marks from it nor
+//! deletes it, stops there with the obligation and its cursor in place, names it on the audit seam,
+//! and the leg answers [`Reconciled::Blocked`]. GC holds every fragment of such a chunk meanwhile
+//! (`StagedSet`'s `untrusted-staged-record`, a malformed committed placement once it is still
+//! referenced), so a hold costs retention, never bytes. Retiring an untrusted staged record by
+//! chunk id instead is the owned walk's (child-2 of #659).
+//!
+//! **Segments before parts** (`0016:2193-2196`; restore's attempt check). For a session token
+//! `s:<id>:<E>` whose `retire:records:` obligation owes `{seg}`, the `retire:bytes:` obligation of
+//! the same token deletes no `part:` record until that records obligation is gone; it may still
+//! write marks. The post-restore pass checks that every surviving segment's chunks are held by a
+//! surviving part (`crate::restore`, `check_attempt`), so deleting the parts first would make an
+//! honest half-drained state read as damage. One keyed read decides it.
+//!
+//! **The `session` component** owes the session's owned `sidx:` residue, which the owned-staging
+//! walk drains by reference (`0016:672`) — not this leg's. An obligation owing it is drained only
+//! while that range is observed empty (one bounded read; no owned entry is created once the
+//! session has left `Open`, X43), and is otherwise held, naming the range.
+//!
+//! **Bounds.** Each step's commit holds at most [`MAX_BATCH_BYTES`] and [`MAX_BATCH_OPS`] —
+//! preconditions counted, the obligation's own `require` and put included — and reads at most
+//! [`POSITIONS_PER_STEP`] marks. A pass commits at most [`STEPS_PER_PASS`] steps over every
+//! obligation together, and reads `retire:` in pages of [`OBLIGATION_PAGE`]. No read covers more
+//! than one obligation's own bounded ranges.
+//!
+//! **A step that loses, or whose outcome is unknown.** A step that answers `Conflict` lost to a
+//! concurrent writer of the obligation or of a mark it read; the drain re-reads the obligation and
+//! plans afresh from the store, at most [`LOST_STEPS_PER_PASS`] times an obligation a pass. A
+//! commit that answers an error — a store fault, or an outcome the store could not report — ends
+//! the pass with that error, never as a `Conflict`: the next pass reads whichever way it went. A
+//! step that landed moved the obligation's bytes, and a step planned on the old bytes then loses
+//! its precondition; a mark it wrote names this obligation, so the next step skips it (arm (a)).
+//!
+//! **One clock.** A mark's `orphaned_at` is the step's `now_millis`, the reading GC ages the same
+//! mark on (ADR-0009): grace for drained bytes starts at the drain (`0016:1098-1104`).
+//!
+//! Dependency boundary (ADR-0010): `traits` / `core` seams plus `tracing`, no concrete backend.
+
+use std::collections::HashSet;
+
+use bytes::Bytes;
+use wyrd_core::metadata::{
+    decode_orphan_mark, encode_orphan_mark, parse_seg_key, seg_key, seg_range_prefix, ChunkRef,
+    OrphanMark, SegmentGroup, SegmentRecord, MAX_ORPHAN_EVENT_LEN, MAX_SEGMENT_INDEX,
+};
+use wyrd_core::multipart::{
+    decode_part_record, decode_retire_obligation, parse_part_key, part_key, part_range, psum_key,
+    retire_key, sidx_range, PartNumber, PartScope, RetireCursor, RetireMode, RetirePayload,
+    RetireToken, RetireWork, RetiredMap, UploadId, MAX_BATCH_BYTES, MAX_BATCH_OPS, MAX_PART_NUMBER,
+};
+use wyrd_traits::{CommitOutcome, DServerId, FragmentId, MetadataStore, Result, WriteBatch};
+
+use crate::gc::{self, checked_page, object_name, orphan_key};
+use crate::reconciliation::Reconciled;
+
+/// Every fragment position a chunk list places, in order, or why its placement cannot be trusted.
+type Positions = std::result::Result<Vec<(DServerId, FragmentId)>, String>;
+
+/// The `retire:` ledger's prefix: both modes, bytes first in key order.
+const RETIRE_PREFIX: &[u8] = b"retire:";
+
+/// **S** — the most drain steps (commits) one pass makes, over every obligation together.
+///
+/// Derivation: a step carries at most [`MAX_BATCH_OPS`] (500) sequential operations, which
+/// `0016:640-648` sizes to finish inside one transaction's five seconds on the slowest supported
+/// backend at an assumed 5 ms each. Sixteen of them are at most 8,000 operations a pass — about
+/// forty seconds at that assumed latency, so a pass stays bounded in time whatever the ledger
+/// holds. At that rate a `{parts}` obligation over the 10,000 parts `MAX_PARTS_PER_SESSION`
+/// allows drains in a handful of passes; a maximum segmented generation (~1.78 M marks,
+/// `0016:1094`) takes about 450.
+pub(crate) const STEPS_PER_PASS: usize = 16;
+
+/// **P** — how many obligations one page of the walk of `retire:` reads. Each value is at most
+/// one [`wyrd_core::metadata::MAX_VALUE_BYTES`], so a page holds at most 6.4 MB.
+pub(crate) const OBLIGATION_PAGE: usize = 64;
+
+/// The most `part:` / `seg:` records one page of a step's range read holds: 64 values of at most
+/// 100 KB each. A step deletes at most [`MAX_BATCH_OPS`] records, so it reads at most eight pages.
+const RECORD_PAGE: usize = 64;
+
+/// The most `orphan:` marks one step reads. A position whose mark already names this obligation
+/// costs no operation in the commit (arm (a)), so the operation budget alone would not bound a
+/// step's reads over a range that is already marked; this does.
+pub(crate) const POSITIONS_PER_STEP: usize = MAX_BATCH_OPS as usize;
+
+/// How often one obligation's step may lose its precondition in one pass before the pass leaves
+/// it to the next: each loss re-reads and re-derives, and a mark that changed under the step is
+/// then judged afresh.
+const LOST_STEPS_PER_PASS: usize = 2;
+
+/// The bytes a cursor adds to an obligation's value at most:
+/// `,"cursor":{"component":"generation","entry":4294967295,"mark":4294967295}` is 73.
+const CURSOR_BYTES: u64 = 80;
+
+/// The widest `orphan:` value a step can read: a decodable mark is at most 328 bytes
+/// ([`MAX_ORPHAN_EVENT_LEN`] plus the codec's constant fields, pinned by core's codec tests).
+const MAX_MARK_BYTES: u64 = MAX_ORPHAN_EVENT_LEN as u64 + 72;
+
+/// One pass of the retirement leg over `meta` at `now_millis`. Dispatched only from
+/// [`crate::reconcile_step`], never a parallel entry.
+///
+/// Answers [`Reconciled::Blocked`] when an obligation is held on something it cannot trust (see
+/// the module doc), [`Reconciled::Changed`] when any step landed, [`Reconciled::Partial`] when
+/// work remains that this pass did not do — its step budget ran out, a step lost its
+/// precondition, or an obligation waits on GC, on a records obligation or on owned residue — and
+/// [`Reconciled::Satisfied`] when the ledger holds nothing to drain.
+pub(crate) async fn reconcile(meta: &dyn MetadataStore, now_millis: u64) -> Result<Reconciled> {
+    let mut leg = Leg {
+        meta,
+        now_millis,
+        steps_left: STEPS_PER_PASS,
+        changed: false,
+        partial: false,
+        blocked: false,
+    };
+    let mut after: Option<Vec<u8>> = None;
+    'walk: loop {
+        let (page, next) = checked_page(
+            meta,
+            RETIRE_PREFIX,
+            "retire-ledger",
+            after.as_deref(),
+            OBLIGATION_PAGE,
+        )
+        .await?;
+        for (key, value) in &page {
+            if leg.steps_left == 0 {
+                leg.partial = true;
+                break 'walk;
+            }
+            leg.drain(key, value.clone()).await?;
+        }
+        match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+            (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+            _ => break,
+        }
+    }
+    Ok(leg.answer())
+}
+
+/// One pass's state across the obligations it drains.
+struct Leg<'m> {
+    meta: &'m dyn MetadataStore,
+    now_millis: u64,
+    /// Steps this pass may still commit.
+    steps_left: usize,
+    /// Whether any step landed.
+    changed: bool,
+    /// Whether work remains that this pass left for the next.
+    partial: bool,
+    /// Whether an obligation is held on something it cannot trust.
+    blocked: bool,
+}
+
+impl Leg<'_> {
+    /// The least certified answer this pass can give ([`Reconciled`]'s ranking).
+    fn answer(&self) -> Reconciled {
+        if self.blocked {
+            Reconciled::Blocked
+        } else if self.changed {
+            Reconciled::Changed
+        } else if self.partial {
+            Reconciled::Partial
+        } else {
+            Reconciled::Satisfied
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Drain the obligation at `key`, read as `value`, step after step until it is finished, held,
+    /// or this pass's budget is spent.
+    async fn drain(&mut self, key: &[u8], mut value: Bytes) -> Result<()> {
+        let mut lost = 0;
+        loop {
+            if self.steps_left == 0 {
+                self.partial = true;
+                return Ok(());
+            }
+            let plan = Step::plan(self.meta, key, &value, self.now_millis).await?;
+            if let Some(commit) = plan.commit {
+                self.steps_left -= 1;
+                match self.meta.commit(commit.batch).await? {
+                    CommitOutcome::Committed => {
+                        self.changed = true;
+                        emit_step(key, plan.marks, plan.deletes, commit.next.is_none());
+                        match commit.next {
+                            Some(next) => value = next,
+                            None => return Ok(()),
+                        }
+                    }
+                    CommitOutcome::Conflict => {
+                        // The obligation moved, or a mark changed after the step read it. Never a
+                        // blind retry of what it planned: re-read and re-derive, so a mark now
+                        // `reclaiming` is judged as arm (0), never re-stamped as (b).
+                        emit_lost(key);
+                        lost += 1;
+                        if lost > LOST_STEPS_PER_PASS {
+                            self.partial = true;
+                            return Ok(());
+                        }
+                        match self.meta.get(key).await? {
+                            Some(current) => value = current,
+                            // Finished by another drainer: nothing left to do.
+                            None => return Ok(()),
+                        }
+                        continue;
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+            match plan.hold {
+                Some(hold) => {
+                    emit_hold(key, &hold);
+                    if hold.needs_human() {
+                        self.blocked = true;
+                    } else {
+                        self.partial = true;
+                    }
+                    return Ok(());
+                }
+                // A step with neither a commit nor a hold has nothing left to do here; a step that
+                // committed and is not finished goes on.
+                None if plan.progressed => {}
+                None => return Ok(()),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// Why a step stopped short of its obligation's end without a budget to blame.
+#[derive(Debug)]
+enum Hold {
+    /// The obligation does not decode against its key: a key the drain cannot parse, or a value
+    /// it cannot read (`0016:438-440`). Never acted on as a default.
+    UnreadableObligation { fault: String },
+    /// A record the obligation names cannot be trusted: it will not decode, its key is not one of
+    /// its range's, or a chunk's placement is not one D server per fragment. Neither marked from
+    /// nor deleted.
+    UntrustedRecord { record: Vec<u8>, fault: String },
+    /// A position's mark is none of the three shapes: left byte-identical, as GC leaves it.
+    UnreadableMark { mark: Vec<u8>, fault: String },
+    /// The cursor's mark lies past the work of the record it points into.
+    CursorPastRecord {
+        record: Vec<u8>,
+        mark: u32,
+        total: usize,
+    },
+    /// Arm (0): a position's mark is `reclaiming`, GC's decision to reclaim. Left byte-identical;
+    /// the drain resumes once GC's key delete lands.
+    Reclaiming { mark: Vec<u8> },
+    /// The same token's `retire:records:` obligation still owes segments: no `part:` record is
+    /// deleted until it is gone.
+    SegmentsFirst { records: Vec<u8> },
+    /// The session still holds owned `sidx:` residue, which the owned-staging walk drains.
+    OwnedResidue { range: Vec<u8> },
+}
+
+impl Hold {
+    /// Whether the hold waits on a record nothing in this tree repairs — a human's — rather than
+    /// on work another pass, or another loop, does.
+    fn needs_human(&self) -> bool {
+        matches!(
+            self,
+            Self::UnreadableObligation { .. }
+                | Self::UntrustedRecord { .. }
+                | Self::UnreadableMark { .. }
+                | Self::CursorPastRecord { .. }
+        )
+    }
+
+    /// The audit reason.
+    fn reason(&self) -> &'static str {
+        match self {
+            Self::UnreadableObligation { .. } => "unreadable-obligation",
+            Self::UntrustedRecord { .. } => "untrusted-record",
+            Self::UnreadableMark { .. } => "unreadable-mark",
+            Self::CursorPastRecord { .. } => "cursor-past-record",
+            Self::Reclaiming { .. } => "reclaiming-mark",
+            Self::SegmentsFirst { .. } => "segments-first",
+            Self::OwnedResidue { .. } => "owned-residue",
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// The record the hold names, and what is wrong with it.
+    fn detail(&self) -> (String, String) {
+        match self {
+            Self::UnreadableObligation { fault } => (String::new(), fault.clone()),
+            Self::UntrustedRecord { record, fault }
+            | Self::UnreadableMark {
+                mark: record,
+                fault,
+            } => (object_name(record), fault.clone()),
+            Self::CursorPastRecord {
+                record,
+                mark,
+                total,
+            } => (
+                object_name(record),
+                format!("the cursor is at mark {mark} of a record with {total} positions"),
+            ),
+            Self::Reclaiming { mark } => (object_name(mark), String::new()),
+            Self::SegmentsFirst { records } => (object_name(records), String::new()),
+            Self::OwnedResidue { range } => (object_name(range), String::new()),
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+/// What one step decided: the commit it makes, if any, and why it stopped, if it stopped short.
+struct Plan {
+    commit: Option<Commit>,
+    hold: Option<Hold>,
+    /// Whether the step moved past anything — wrote, deleted, or advanced its cursor.
+    progressed: bool,
+    marks: usize,
+    deletes: usize,
+}
+
+/// A step's batch, and the obligation's value once it lands (`None`: the step deletes it).
+struct Commit {
+    batch: WriteBatch,
+    next: Option<Bytes>,
+}
+
+/// What one fragment position came to.
+enum Marked {
+    /// Marked in this step's batch, or already carrying this obligation's event.
+    Done,
+    /// The step's budget cannot take it: the step stops before it.
+    Budget,
+    /// The position holds the step there.
+    Held(Hold),
+}
+
+/// How far one kind of work got.
+enum Flow {
+    /// Every entry of it is done.
+    Done,
+    /// Stopped at this cursor — for the budget, or for the hold the step recorded.
+    Stop(RetireCursor),
+}
+
+/// The bytes and operations a step's commit may still take.
+struct Budget {
+    ops: u64,
+    bytes: u64,
+}
+
+impl Budget {
+    /// The budget left once the obligation's own two operations are reserved: its
+    /// `require(== prior)`, and the put of its advanced value (or its delete, which is smaller).
+    fn for_obligation(key: &[u8], prior: &[u8]) -> Self {
+        let own = 2 * key.len() as u64 + 2 * prior.len() as u64 + CURSOR_BYTES;
+        Self {
+            ops: u64::from(MAX_BATCH_OPS).saturating_sub(2),
+            bytes: MAX_BATCH_BYTES.saturating_sub(own),
+        }
+    }
+
+    fn fits(&self, ops: u64, bytes: u64) -> bool {
+        ops <= self.ops && bytes <= self.bytes
+    }
+
+    fn take(&mut self, ops: u64, bytes: u64) -> bool {
+        if !self.fits(ops, bytes) {
+            return false;
+        }
+        self.ops -= ops;
+        self.bytes -= bytes;
+        true
+    }
+}
+
+/// One drain step over one obligation, as it is being planned.
+struct Step<'s> {
+    meta: &'s dyn MetadataStore,
+    mode: RetireMode,
+    token: RetireToken,
+    payload: RetirePayload,
+    /// The event identity every mark this step writes carries: the obligation's token
+    /// (`0016:1176`), which is what GC's keyed protection looks the obligation up by.
+    event: String,
+    /// The mark this step writes at every position it marks.
+    fresh: Bytes,
+    budget: Budget,
+    batch: WriteBatch,
+    /// The `orphan:` keys this step's batch already marks, so a position named twice is marked
+    /// once.
+    planned: HashSet<Vec<u8>>,
+    positions_read: usize,
+    marks: usize,
+    deletes: usize,
+    progressed: bool,
+    hold: Option<Hold>,
+    /// The same token's records obligation still owes segments — read once a step.
+    segments_first: Option<bool>,
+}
+
+impl<'s> Step<'s> {
+    /// Plan one step over the obligation at `key`, read as `prior`.
+    async fn plan(
+        meta: &'s dyn MetadataStore,
+        key: &[u8],
+        prior: &Bytes,
+        now_millis: u64,
+    ) -> Result<Plan> {
+        let held = |hold| Plan {
+            commit: None,
+            hold: Some(hold),
+            progressed: false,
+            marks: 0,
+            deletes: 0,
+        };
+        let (mode, token, payload) = match decode_retire_obligation(key, prior) {
+            Ok(decoded) => decoded,
+            Err(fault) => {
+                let fault = fault.to_string();
+                return Ok(held(Hold::UnreadableObligation { fault }));
+            }
+        };
+        let event = token.to_string();
+        let fresh = match OrphanMark::structured(now_millis, event.clone()) {
+            Ok(mark) => encode_orphan_mark(&mark),
+            Err(fault) => {
+                let fault = fault.to_string();
+                return Ok(held(Hold::UnreadableObligation { fault }));
+            }
+        };
+        let mut step = Step {
+            meta,
+            mode,
+            token,
+            payload,
+            event,
+            fresh,
+            budget: Budget::for_obligation(key, prior),
+            batch: WriteBatch::new(),
+            planned: HashSet::new(),
+            positions_read: 0,
+            marks: 0,
+            deletes: 0,
+            progressed: false,
+            hold: None,
+            segments_first: None,
+        };
+        let stopped = step.run().await?;
+        step.finish(key, prior, stopped)
+    }
+
+    /// Walk the payload's ordered work from its cursor. `None` when all of it is done; else where
+    /// the step stopped.
+    async fn run(&mut self) -> Result<Option<RetireCursor>> {
+        if self.payload.session() {
+            if let Some(range) = self.owned_residue().await? {
+                self.hold = Some(Hold::OwnedResidue { range });
+                return Ok(self.payload.cursor().copied().or(Some(self.start())));
+            }
+        }
+        let work: Vec<RetireWork> = self.payload.work().collect();
+        let resume = self.payload.cursor().copied();
+        let first = resume.map_or(0, |cursor| {
+            work.iter()
+                .position(|w| *w == cursor.component())
+                .unwrap_or(0)
+        });
+        for (index, &kind) in work.iter().enumerate().skip(first) {
+            let from = resume.filter(|_| index == first);
+            let flow = match kind {
+                RetireWork::Seg => self.seg(from).await?,
+                RetireWork::Parts => self.parts(from).await?,
+                RetireWork::Chunks => self.chunks(from).await?,
+                RetireWork::Generation => self.generation(from).await?,
+            };
+            match flow {
+                Flow::Done => self.progressed = true,
+                Flow::Stop(cursor) => return Ok(Some(cursor)),
+            }
+        }
+        Ok(None)
+    }
+
+    /// Where the payload's work starts — the position an absent cursor means.
+    fn start(&self) -> RetireCursor {
+        let kind = self.payload.work().next().unwrap_or(RetireWork::Parts);
+        let entry = match (kind, self.payload.parts()) {
+            (RetireWork::Parts, Some(PartScope::Set(set))) => set.runs()[0].0,
+            (RetireWork::Parts, _) => 1,
+            _ => 0,
+        };
+        RetireCursor::new(kind, entry, 0)
+    }
+
+    /// Turn what the walk reached into the step's commit. `stopped` is `None` when every entry is
+    /// done: the obligation is deleted, in this commit. Otherwise its cursor moves there — and a
+    /// step that wrote nothing and moved nothing commits nothing.
+    fn finish(self, key: &[u8], prior: &Bytes, stopped: Option<RetireCursor>) -> Result<Plan> {
+        let Step {
+            mode,
+            payload,
+            batch,
+            marks,
+            deletes,
+            hold,
+            progressed,
+            ..
+        } = self;
+        let mutated = marks + deletes > 0;
+        let (batch, next) = match stopped {
+            None => (batch.delete(key.to_vec()), None),
+            Some(cursor) => {
+                // Nothing moved: the stored cursor is where the step stopped, or — with none
+                // stored — the walk never got past the first thing it read.
+                let unchanged = match payload.cursor() {
+                    Some(stored) => *stored == cursor,
+                    None => !progressed,
+                };
+                if unchanged && !mutated {
+                    return Ok(Plan {
+                        commit: None,
+                        hold,
+                        progressed: false,
+                        marks,
+                        deletes,
+                    });
+                }
+                let advanced = match payload.with_cursor(mode, cursor) {
+                    Ok(advanced) => advanced,
+                    // A cursor this step computed that its own decode would refuse: never stored.
+                    Err(fault) => {
+                        let fault = fault.to_string();
+                        return Ok(Plan {
+                            commit: None,
+                            hold: Some(Hold::UnreadableObligation { fault }),
+                            progressed: false,
+                            marks: 0,
+                            deletes: 0,
+                        });
+                    }
+                };
+                let value = wyrd_core::metadata::encode(&advanced);
+                (batch.put(key.to_vec(), value.clone()), Some(value))
+            }
+        };
+        // The obligation's own precondition goes first: single-winner on the obligation.
+        let mut ordered = WriteBatch::new().require(key.to_vec(), prior.clone());
+        ordered.preconditions.extend(batch.preconditions);
+        ordered.puts = batch.puts;
+        ordered.deletes = batch.deletes;
+        Ok(Plan {
+            commit: Some(Commit {
+                batch: ordered,
+                next,
+            }),
+            hold,
+            progressed: true,
+            marks,
+            deletes,
+        })
+    }
+
+    /// The session's owned `sidx:` range, if it still holds an entry: one bounded read.
+    async fn owned_residue(&self) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>> {
+        let RetireToken::Session { upload_id, .. } = &self.token else {
+            return Ok(None);
+        };
+        let range = sidx_range(upload_id);
+        let (page, _) = checked_page(self.meta, &range, "retire-owned-residue", None, 1).await?;
+        Ok((!page.is_empty()).then_some(range))
+    }
+
+    /// The session token this payload's parts belong to.
+    fn upload(&self) -> Option<&UploadId> {
+        match &self.token {
+            RetireToken::Session { upload_id, .. } => Some(upload_id),
+            RetireToken::Generation { .. } => None,
+        }
+    }
+
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // `seg` — a rolled-back attempt's segment records (records only)
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+    async fn seg(&mut self, from: Option<RetireCursor>) -> Result<Flow> {
+        let Some(group) = self.payload.segments().cloned() else {
+            return Ok(Flow::Done);
+        };
+        let mut entry = from.map_or(0, |cursor| cursor.entry());
+        let range = seg_range_prefix(&group);
+        let mut after = segment_before(&group, entry);
+        loop {
+            let (page, next) = checked_page(
+                self.meta,
+                &range,
+                "retire-seg",
+                after.as_deref(),
+                RECORD_PAGE,
+            )
+            .await?;
+            for (key, _) in &page {
+                let index = match segment_index(&group, key) {
+                    Ok(index) => index,
+                    Err(fault) => {
+                        self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord {
+                            record: key.clone(),
+                            fault,
+                        });
+                        return Ok(Flow::Stop(seg_cursor(RetireWork::Seg, entry, 0)));
+                    }
+                };
+                if !self.budget.take(1, key.len() as u64) {
+                    return Ok(Flow::Stop(seg_cursor(RetireWork::Seg, index, 0)));
+                }
+                self.batch = std::mem::take(&mut self.batch).delete(key.clone());
+                self.deletes += 1;
+                entry = index.saturating_add(1);
+            }
+            match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(Flow::Done),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // `parts` — part records: marked then deleted (bytes), deleted (records)
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+    async fn parts(&mut self, from: Option<RetireCursor>) -> Result<Flow> {
+        let (Some(scope), Some(upload)) = (self.payload.parts().cloned(), self.upload().cloned())
+        else {
+            return Ok(Flow::Done);
+        };
+        let (entry, mark) = from.map_or((None, 0), |cursor| (Some(cursor.entry()), cursor.mark()));
+        match (self.mode, scope) {
+            (RetireMode::Records, PartScope::Set(set)) => {
+                let mut next = set.first_from(entry.unwrap_or(0));
+                while let Some(number) = next {
+                    let part = part_number(number);
+                    let (part_key, psum_key) = (part_key(&upload, part), psum_key(&upload, part));
+                    let bytes = (part_key.len() + psum_key.len()) as u64;
+                    if !self.budget.take(2, bytes) {
+                        return Ok(Flow::Stop(RetireCursor::new(RetireWork::Parts, number, 0)));
+                    }
+                    let batch = std::mem::take(&mut self.batch);
+                    self.batch = batch.delete(part_key).delete(psum_key);
+                    self.deletes += 2;
+                    next = number.checked_add(1).and_then(|n| set.first_from(n));
+                }
+                Ok(Flow::Done)
+            }
+            // `all` is a `retire:bytes:` wildcard only (decode refuses it under records).
+            (RetireMode::Records, PartScope::All) => Ok(Flow::Done),
+            (RetireMode::Bytes, PartScope::Set(set)) => {
+                let mut next = set.first_from(entry.unwrap_or(0));
+                let mut mark = mark;
+                while let Some(number) = next {
+                    let key = part_key(&upload, part_number(number));
+                    let value = self.meta.get(&key).await?;
+                    match self.staged_part(&upload, number, &key, value, mark).await? {
+                        Flow::Done => {}
+                        stop => return Ok(stop),
+                    }
+                    mark = 0;
+                    next = number.checked_add(1).and_then(|n| set.first_from(n));
+                }
+                Ok(Flow::Done)
+            }
+            (RetireMode::Bytes, PartScope::All) => {
+                let range = part_range(&upload);
+                let mut after = entry
+                    .and_then(|n| n.checked_sub(1))
+                    .and_then(|n| PartNumber::new(n).ok())
+                    .map(|n| part_key(&upload, n));
+                // The next part number owed, and how much of it is already marked.
+                let (mut next_part, mut mark) = (entry.unwrap_or(1), mark);
+                loop {
+                    let (page, next) = checked_page(
+                        self.meta,
+                        &range,
+                        "retire-parts",
+                        after.as_deref(),
+                        RECORD_PAGE,
+                    )
+                    .await?;
+                    for (key, value) in &page {
+                        let number = match parse_part_key(key) {
+                            Ok((_, number)) => number.get(),
+                            Err(fault) => {
+                                self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord {
+                                    record: key.clone(),
+                                    fault: fault.to_string(),
+                                });
+                                return Ok(Flow::Stop(RetireCursor::new(
+                                    RetireWork::Parts,
+                                    next_part,
+                                    mark,
+                                )));
+                            }
+                        };
+                        let at = if number == next_part { mark } else { 0 };
+                        match self
+                            .staged_part(&upload, number, key, Some(value.clone()), at)
+                            .await?
+                        {
+                            Flow::Done => {}
+                            stop => return Ok(stop),
+                        }
+                        (next_part, mark) = (number.saturating_add(1).min(MAX_PART_NUMBER), 0);
+                    }
+                    match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                        (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                        _ => return Ok(Flow::Done),
+                    }
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Retire one staged part under `retire:bytes:`: mark every fragment its record places, from
+    /// `mark` on, then delete its `part:` and `psum:` records — never the records before the marks,
+    /// never the records while the same token's segments are still owed, and never anything of a
+    /// record whose placement cannot be trusted.
+    async fn staged_part(
+        &mut self,
+        upload: &UploadId,
+        number: u32,
+        key: &[u8],
+        value: Option<Bytes>,
+        mark: u32,
+    ) -> Result<Flow> {
+        let here = |mark| Flow::Stop(RetireCursor::new(RetireWork::Parts, number, mark));
+        let summary = psum_key(upload, part_number(number));
+        let Some(value) = value else {
+            // A member of the set with no record. Every member was a committed part when the set
+            // was frozen, and only this obligation's own step deletes one, in the commit that
+            // moves its cursor past it — so an absent record is damage, and which bytes it named
+            // is unknown. Held and named, never passed over as drained.
+            let (record, fault) = (key.to_vec(), "the part this obligation names has no record");
+            let fault = fault.to_string();
+            self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord { record, fault });
+            return Ok(here(mark));
+        };
+        let record = match decode_part_record(&value) {
+            Ok(record) => record,
+            Err(fault) => {
+                let (record, fault) = (key.to_vec(), fault.to_string());
+                self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord { record, fault });
+                return Ok(here(mark));
+            }
+        };
+        let positions = match staged_positions(record.chunks()) {
+            Ok(positions) => positions,
+            Err(fault) => {
+                self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord {
+                    record: key.to_vec(),
+                    fault,
+                });
+                return Ok(here(mark));
+            }
+        };
+        match self.mark_from(key, &positions, mark).await? {
+            Ok(()) => {}
+            Err(reached) => return Ok(here(reached)),
+        }
+        let total = positions.len() as u32;
+        if let Some(records) = self.segments_owed().await? {
+            self.hold = Some(Hold::SegmentsFirst { records });
+            return Ok(here(total));
+        }
+        if !self.budget.take(2, (key.len() + summary.len()) as u64) {
+            return Ok(here(total));
+        }
+        self.batch = std::mem::take(&mut self.batch)
+            .delete(key.to_vec())
+            .delete(summary);
+        self.deletes += 2;
+        Ok(Flow::Done)
+    }
+
+    /// The same token's `retire:records:` obligation, if it still owes segments (or will not
+    /// decode, so it may). One keyed read a step. No writer installs that key once the fence that
+    /// installed this obligation beside it has landed — a token is minted once (`0016:358-373`) —
+    /// so an absence read here is final.
+    async fn segments_owed(&mut self) -> Result<Option<Vec<u8>>> {
+        let records = match &self.token {
+            RetireToken::Session { part: None, .. } => retire_key(RetireMode::Records, &self.token),
+            _ => return Ok(None),
+        };
+        let owed = match self.segments_first {
+            Some(owed) => owed,
+            None => {
+                let owed = match self.meta.get(&records).await? {
+                    None => false,
+                    Some(value) => match decode_retire_obligation(&records, &value) {
+                        Ok((_, _, payload)) => payload.segments().is_some(),
+                        Err(_) => true,
+                    },
+                };
+                self.segments_first = Some(owed);
+                owed
+            }
+        };
+        Ok(owed.then_some(records))
+    }
+
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // `chunks` — a per-part obligation's staged chunks (marks only)
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+    async fn chunks(&mut self, from: Option<RetireCursor>) -> Result<Flow> {
+        let chunks = self.payload.chunks().to_vec();
+        self.chunk_list(RetireWork::Chunks, &chunks, from, staged_positions)
+            .await
+    }
+
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // `generation` — a superseded or deleted generation's committed chunks
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+    async fn generation(&mut self, from: Option<RetireCursor>) -> Result<Flow> {
+        let Some(generation) = self.payload.generation().cloned() else {
+            return Ok(Flow::Done);
+        };
+        match generation.map() {
+            RetiredMap::Flat(chunks) => {
+                self.chunk_list(RetireWork::Generation, chunks, from, committed_positions)
+                    .await
+            }
+            RetiredMap::Segmented(group) => self.segments(group, from).await,
+        }
+    }
+
+    /// Mark a chunk list no record names, one chunk an entry: `positions` resolves each chunk's
+    /// placement by the rule its origin is held to.
+    async fn chunk_list(
+        &mut self,
+        kind: RetireWork,
+        chunks: &[ChunkRef],
+        from: Option<RetireCursor>,
+        positions: fn(&[ChunkRef]) -> Positions,
+    ) -> Result<Flow> {
+        let (first, mut mark) = from.map_or((0, 0), |cursor| (cursor.entry(), cursor.mark()));
+        let key = self_key(&self.token, self.mode);
+        for (index, chunk) in chunks.iter().enumerate().skip(first as usize) {
+            let entry = index as u32;
+            let at = |mark| Flow::Stop(RetireCursor::new(kind, entry, mark));
+            let placed = match positions(std::slice::from_ref(chunk)) {
+                Ok(placed) => placed,
+                Err(fault) => {
+                    self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord {
+                        record: key.clone(),
+                        fault: format!("chunk {}: {fault}", wyrd_traits::chunk_hex(chunk.id)),
+                    });
+                    return Ok(at(mark));
+                }
+            };
+            match self.mark_from(&key, &placed, mark).await? {
+                Ok(()) => {}
+                Err(reached) => return Ok(at(reached)),
+            }
+            mark = 0;
+        }
+        Ok(Flow::Done)
+    }
+
+    /// Retire a segmented generation's `seg:` records: each record's **current** placement is read
+    /// at drain time and marked (`0016:2417-2425`), then the record is deleted.
+    async fn segments(&mut self, group: &SegmentGroup, from: Option<RetireCursor>) -> Result<Flow> {
+        let (mut entry, mut mark) = from.map_or((0, 0), |cursor| (cursor.entry(), cursor.mark()));
+        let range = seg_range_prefix(group);
+        let mut after = segment_before(group, entry);
+        loop {
+            let (page, next) = checked_page(
+                self.meta,
+                &range,
+                "retire-generation",
+                after.as_deref(),
+                RECORD_PAGE,
+            )
+            .await?;
+            for (key, value) in &page {
+                let stop =
+                    |entry, mark| Flow::Stop(seg_cursor(RetireWork::Generation, entry, mark));
+                let index = match segment_index(group, key) {
+                    Ok(index) => index,
+                    Err(fault) => {
+                        let record = key.clone();
+                        self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord { record, fault });
+                        return Ok(stop(entry, mark));
+                    }
+                };
+                let at = if index == entry { mark } else { 0 };
+                let decoded = wyrd_core::metadata::decode::<SegmentRecord>(value)
+                    .map_err(|fault| fault.to_string())
+                    .and_then(|segment| committed_positions(segment.chunks()));
+                let positions = match decoded {
+                    Ok(positions) => positions,
+                    Err(fault) => {
+                        let record = key.clone();
+                        self.hold = Some(Hold::UntrustedRecord { record, fault });
+                        return Ok(stop(index, at));
+                    }
+                };
+                match self.mark_from(key, &positions, at).await? {
+                    Ok(()) => {}
+                    Err(reached) => return Ok(stop(index, reached)),
+                }
+                if !self.budget.take(1, key.len() as u64) {
+                    return Ok(stop(index, positions.len() as u32));
+                }
+                self.batch = std::mem::take(&mut self.batch).delete(key.clone());
+                self.deletes += 1;
+                (entry, mark) = (index.saturating_add(1), 0);
+            }
+            match (next, page.into_iter().last()) {
+                (Some(_), Some((last, _))) => after = Some(last),
+                _ => return Ok(Flow::Done),
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+    // The mark guard
+    // ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
+
+    /// Mark `positions` from index `mark` on, in order. `Ok(Err(reached))` when the step stops at
+    /// position `reached` — for the budget, or for the hold it records.
+    async fn mark_from(
+        &mut self,
+        record: &[u8],
+        positions: &[(DServerId, FragmentId)],
+        mark: u32,
+    ) -> Result<std::result::Result<(), u32>> {
+        let start = mark as usize;
+        if start > positions.len() {
+            self.hold = Some(Hold::CursorPastRecord {
+                record: record.to_vec(),
+                mark,
+                total: positions.len(),
+            });
+            return Ok(Err(mark));
+        }
+        for (offset, &(dserver, frag)) in positions.iter().enumerate().skip(start) {
+            match self.mark(dserver, frag).await? {
+                Marked::Done => self.progressed = true,
+                Marked::Budget => return Ok(Err(offset as u32)),
+                Marked::Held(hold) => {
+                    self.hold = Some(hold);
+                    return Ok(Err(offset as u32));
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        Ok(Ok(()))
+    }
+
+    /// One position through the guard: arm (0), (a), (b) or (c) (module doc).
+    async fn mark(&mut self, dserver: DServerId, frag: FragmentId) -> Result<Marked> {
+        let key = orphan_key(dserver, frag);
+        if self.planned.contains(&key) {
+            return Ok(Marked::Done);
+        }
+        let worst = 2 * key.len() as u64 + MAX_MARK_BYTES + self.fresh.len() as u64;
+        if self.positions_read >= POSITIONS_PER_STEP || !self.budget.fits(2, worst) {
+            return Ok(Marked::Budget);
+        }
+        self.positions_read += 1;
+        let current = self.meta.get(&key).await?;
+        let batch = std::mem::take(&mut self.batch);
+        self.batch = match current {
+            // (c) absent: written under `require_absent`.
+            None => {
+                let bytes = 2 * key.len() as u64 + self.fresh.len() as u64;
+                self.budget.take(2, bytes);
+                batch
+                    .require_absent(key.clone())
+                    .put(key.clone(), self.fresh.clone())
+            }
+            Some(current) => match decode_orphan_mark(&current) {
+                Err(fault) => {
+                    self.batch = batch;
+                    let fault = fault.to_string();
+                    return Ok(Marked::Held(Hold::UnreadableMark { mark: key, fault }));
+                }
+                // (0) `reclaiming`, whatever its event: left byte-identical.
+                Ok(found) if found.is_reclaiming() => {
+                    self.batch = batch;
+                    return Ok(Marked::Held(Hold::Reclaiming { mark: key }));
+                }
+                // (a) this retirement's own mark: its stamp stays.
+                Ok(found) if found.event() == Some(self.event.as_str()) => {
+                    self.batch = batch;
+                    return Ok(Marked::Done);
+                }
+                // (b) another event, or a legacy mark: re-stamped under the exact prior value.
+                Ok(_) => {
+                    let bytes = 2 * key.len() as u64 + (current.len() + self.fresh.len()) as u64;
+                    self.budget.take(2, bytes);
+                    batch
+                        .require(key.clone(), current)
+                        .put(key.clone(), self.fresh.clone())
+                }
+            },
+        };
+        self.planned.insert(key);
+        self.marks += 1;
+        Ok(Marked::Done)
+    }
+}
+
+/// The key of the obligation a step works on, rebuilt from its decoded halves for naming a hold.
+fn self_key(token: &RetireToken, mode: RetireMode) -> Vec<u8> {
+    retire_key(mode, token)
+}
+
+/// `number` as a part number. Every number a drain names is a member of a decoded set or the
+/// number of a parsed `part:` key, so it is in range; one that is not falls back to the format
+/// maximum, whose key no record holds.
+fn part_number(number: u32) -> PartNumber {
+    PartNumber::new(number)
+        .or_else(|_| PartNumber::new(MAX_PART_NUMBER))
+        .expect("MAX_PART_NUMBER is a part number")
+}
+
+/// The exclusive `after` a range walk of `group`'s segments starts from to reach index `entry`:
+/// the key of the index before it, or `None` from the start.
+fn segment_before(group: &SegmentGroup, entry: u32) -> Option<Vec<u8>> {
+    entry
+        .checked_sub(1)
+        .and_then(|before| seg_key(group, before).ok())
+}
+
+/// The index of a `seg:` key under `group`'s range, or why it is not one of its records.
+fn segment_index(group: &SegmentGroup, key: &[u8]) -> std::result::Result<u32, String> {
+    match parse_seg_key(key) {
+        Ok((nonce, epoch, index)) if &nonce == group.nonce() && epoch == group.epoch() => Ok(index),
+        Ok(_) => Err("a key under the group's range naming another group".to_string()),
+        Err(fault) => Err(fault.to_string()),
+    }
+}
+
+/// A cursor at segment `entry`, held to the format's segment maximum: past the last addressable
+/// index there is no record left to resume at, and decode refuses a cursor beyond it.
+fn seg_cursor(kind: RetireWork, entry: u32, mark: u32) -> RetireCursor {
+    RetireCursor::new(kind, entry.min(MAX_SEGMENT_INDEX), mark)
+}
+
+/// Every fragment position of `chunks`, by the **staged** rule: exactly one D server per fragment
+/// of the chunk's own scheme, the empty vector included as damage ([`gc::staged_placement`]).
+fn staged_positions(chunks: &[ChunkRef]) -> Positions {
+    let mut positions = Vec::new();
+    for chunk in chunks {
+        let fragments = gc::staged_placement(chunk).map_err(|m| {
+            format!(
+                "chunk {}: staged placement names {} D server(s) for a scheme of {} fragment(s)",
+                wyrd_traits::chunk_hex(chunk.id),
+                m.actual,
+                m.expected
+            )
+        })?;
+        positions.extend(fragments.map(|(index, dserver)| {
+            let frag = FragmentId {
+                chunk: chunk.id,
+                index,
+            };
+            (dserver, frag)
+        }));
+    }
+    Ok(positions)
+}
+
+/// Every fragment position of `chunks`, by the **committed** rule ([`ChunkRef::checked_fragments`],
+/// ADR-0040): an empty placement is the identity fallback, any other wrong length is malformed.
+fn committed_positions(chunks: &[ChunkRef]) -> Positions {
+    let mut positions = Vec::new();
+    for chunk in chunks {
+        let fragments = chunk.checked_fragments().map_err(|m| {
+            format!(
+                "chunk {}: committed placement names {} D server(s) for a scheme of {} \
+                 fragment(s)",
+                wyrd_traits::chunk_hex(chunk.id),
+                m.actual,
+                m.expected
+            )
+        })?;
+        positions.extend(fragments.map(|(index, dserver)| {
+            let frag = FragmentId {
+                chunk: chunk.id,
+                index,
+            };
+            (dserver, frag)
+        }));
+    }
+    Ok(positions)
+}
+
+/// A drain step that landed, on the durability-plane seam (ADR-0011 / ADR-0012).
+fn emit_step(obligation: &[u8], marks: usize, deletes: usize, finished: bool) {
+    if marks > 0 {
+        tracing::info!(monotonic_counter.retirement_marks_written = marks as u64);
+    }
+    if deletes > 0 {
+        tracing::info!(monotonic_counter.retirement_records_deleted = deletes as u64);
+    }
+    if finished {
+        tracing::info!(monotonic_counter.retirement_obligations_drained = 1_u64);
+    }
+    tracing::info!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.retirement.audit",
+        action = "drain-step",
+        obligation = %object_name(obligation),
+        marks,
+        deletes,
+        finished,
+        "the retirement drain committed a step: marks written before the records that named their \
+         bytes were deleted",
+    );
+}
+
+/// A step held short of its obligation's end. A hold a human must clear is a warning.
+fn emit_hold(obligation: &[u8], hold: &Hold) {
+    let (record, detail) = hold.detail();
+    let reason = hold.reason();
+    tracing::info!(monotonic_counter.retirement_holds = 1_u64, reason);
+    if hold.needs_human() {
+        tracing::warn!(
+            target: "wyrd.custodian.retirement.audit",
+            action = "hold",
+            reason,
+            obligation = %object_name(obligation),
+            record = %record,
+            detail = %detail,
+            "the retirement drain holds an obligation on a record it cannot trust: nothing of that \
+             record is marked or deleted, the obligation and its cursor stay — operator signal",
+        );
+    } else {
+        tracing::info!(
+            target: "wyrd.custodian.retirement.audit",
+            action = "hold",
+            reason,
+            obligation = %object_name(obligation),
+            record = %record,
+            detail = %detail,
+            "the retirement drain holds an obligation until other work lands; it resumes from its \
+             cursor",
+        );
+    }
+}
+
+/// A step that lost its precondition: re-read and re-derived, never retried blind.
+fn emit_lost(obligation: &[u8]) {
+    tracing::info!(monotonic_counter.retirement_steps_lost = 1_u64);
+    tracing::info!(
+        target: "wyrd.custodian.retirement.audit",
+        action = "step-lost",
+        obligation = %object_name(obligation),
+        "a retirement drain step lost its precondition; the drain re-reads before it plans again",
+    );
+}
diff --git a/crates/custodian/tests/multipart_retirement_drain.rs b/crates/custodian/tests/multipart_retirement_drain.rs
